<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The watch app's themes, and the only reason they exist is the splash screen.

  ## Three rejections, and what each one actually said

  Rejected 2026-09-08, Wear App Quality Guidelines: "Missing app icon in splash
  screen". `:wear` had no theme AT ALL — no <application android:theme>, no
  themes.xml, nothing but a Translucent theme on the activation activity — so
  the launch window had no icon to show and came up bare.

  Rejected 2026-09-10. The first fix put the mark on @color/ic_launcher_background
  — the blush the launcher icon uses — because reproducing the launcher icon
  looked like the coherent choice. The guideline's wording is exact and it is not
  a matter of taste: BLACK.

  Rejected 2026-09-13, against version code 1030, the build that finally had a
  black background. Two things were wrong and both came from writing these
  attributes by hand instead of following Google's Wear splash guide:

    1. The icon was @drawable/ic_launcher_foreground — the adaptive icon's
       FOREGROUND LAYER ONLY. That layer is the mark in #80475C, drawn to sit on
       a blush ground. On black it is dark plum hairlines at roughly 3:1
       contrast with no disc behind them. The guideline says the splash icon
       "must match the app launcher icon", and a launcher icon is both layers.
    2. Nothing sized it. A drawable handed straight to
       windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon is scaled into the system's own slot, so
       "48x48dp" was never asserted anywhere.

  ## So this is Google's construction, copied

  From the Wear OS splash screen guide: androidx.core:core-splashscreen, a
  Theme.SplashScreen child as the LAUNCH theme on the launcher activity,
  postSplashScreenTheme pointing back at the real theme, the icon wrapped in a
  layer-list at @dimen/splash_screen_icon_size, and installSplashScreen() called
  before super.onCreate(). The library is used even though minSdk is 36 — the
  guide calls for 1.0.1 or higher because that is what carries "support for
  default Wear OS dimensions", which the bare platform attributes do not have.

  The earlier note here argued the opposite: that the library bought nothing
  above API 31 and that naming the foreground layer avoided drawing the icon's
  background twice. Both readings were reasonable and both were wrong, and the
  cost of each was a review cycle. Where this page and a reasonable inference
  disagree, follow the page.

  windowBackground is black because this is a watch and the app's own surface is
  dark: without it the window flashes the platform default between the splash
  and the first Compose frame.
-->
<resources>
  <!-- What the app actually wears, once it is running. -->
  <style name="Theme.BfgWatchFaces" parent="@android:style/Theme.DeviceDefault.NoActionBar">
    <item name="android:windowBackground">@android:color/black</item>
  </style>

  <!--
    The LAUNCH theme, on WatchActivity only. These three names are the library's
    own attributes, so they carry no `android:` prefix — prefixing them reads as
    a typo fix and silently drops the splash back to the platform defaults.
  -->
  <style name="Theme.BfgWatchFaces.Starting" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
    <item name="windowSplashScreenBackground">@android:color/black</item>
    <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@drawable/splash_screen</item>
    <item name="postSplashScreenTheme">@style/Theme.BfgWatchFaces</item>
  </style>
</resources>
